  "narrative": "The 2026 Atlanta Braves announced themselves early as a legitimate contender, with timely power from Matt Olson and emerging contributions from Michael Harris II setting an optimistic tone through April and into May. The offense showed real depth, with unlikely heroes like Dominic Smith delivering clutch doubles and Chadwick Tromp coming through in tight spots, while the rotation gave Atlanta reason to believe this could be a special year.\n\nThe summer months brought turbulence, as Raisel Iglesias became a recurring villain in the bullpen story — surrendering devastating home runs to Braden Montgomery and then Juan Soto in back-to-back months that cost Atlanta dearly. Yet the Braves refused to fold. Ozzie Albies answered Iglesias's June meltdown with a go-ahead two-run blast on June 20th, and the team's resilience became a defining trait through a grueling stretch of July games.\n\nAs the calendar turned to August and September, Atlanta caught fire. Michael Harris II cemented himself as one of the game's most exciting players with a series of clutch extra-base hits, Sean Murphy proved a steady force behind the plate, and Ronald Acuña Jr. reminded everyone of his ceiling with a grand slam in mid-September. Ha-Seong Kim's timely singles in the season's final weeks reflected a lineup that had found its identity — balanced, opportunistic, and dangerous. The Braves finished 94-68, earning a postseason berth they felt they deserved.\n\nThe Wild Card round against the Phillies was a rollercoaster. Chris Sale labored on the mound, surrendering key hits to Alec Bohm and Bryson Stott, and J.T. Realmuto delivered a crushing triple that threatened to derail everything. But Austin Riley — whose regular-season home run total had been modest — rose to the moment with a stunning three-run blast that gave Atlanta the lead and nearly sent them through. In the end, however, the Braves fell short, their promising season ending one round too soon and leaving a talented roster hungry for another shot.",
